<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bloemfontein</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1846">1846</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bloemfontein is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, capital of the Free State province and the country's judicial capital. Founded in 1846 by Major Henry Warden as a British post, it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a> and from 1854 of the Orange Free State. It was occupied by the British in 1900, and since 1910 it has been the seat of the country's highest court of appeal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bloemfontein.jpg" alt="Bloemfontein" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: South African Tourism from South Africa · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bloemfontein,_Free_State,_South_Africa_(20349855518).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bloemfontein lies in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, on the southern edge of the Highveld at an elevation of about 1,400 metres, bordering the semi-arid region of the Karoo. The area is generally flat, with occasional hills, called koppies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, and the vegetation is Highveld grassland. Summers are hot and rainfall comes mainly as short afternoon thunderstorms, while winters are cool and dry with frosty nights. The city is also known as Mangaung in Sesotho, meaning place of cheetahs, and as the city of roses, for its abundance of the flowers and an annual rose festival.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bloemfontein was historically a settlement of the Orana and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a>, and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a>. It was officially founded in 1846 as a fort by the British Army major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-douglas-warden">Henry Douglas Warden</a>, as a British outpost in the Transoranje region. That region was occupied by various groups, among them the Orana, Cape Colony Trekboers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> and the Barolong. The Orange River Sovereignty was proclaimed in 1848 with a British Resident at Bloemfontein, and the Orange Free State, an independent Boer republic between the Orange and Vaal rivers, existed from 1854 to 1902.</p><p>On 31 May 1910, exactly eight years after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> signed the Peace Treaty of Vereeniging that ended the Anglo-Boer War, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> became a Union. Disagreement over where the capital should be ended in a compromise that allowed Bloemfontein to host the Appellate Division and become the Union's judicial capital, and it was also given financial compensation.</p><p>Residential segregation in the town began in the 19th century. Ordinance 1 of 1860 decreed that no non-white person, without written permission from the landlord, which was the British government, had the right to occupy urban land in towns that had no municipality. On 3 June 1861 the council marked out separate locations for the black population and for Coloured people, whose inhabitants had to pay a hut tax and a grazing rights tax. This was the basis of the residential segregation later planned by the architects of apartheid, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> began to implement after winning the 1948 election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bloemfontein is the capital and largest city of the Free State province, with 256,185 residents at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> within the Mangaung municipality of 747,431. It remains the seat of the Supreme Court of Appeal, formerly the Appellate Division, and the Constitutional Court has been in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> since 1994. The city has the Anglo-Boer War Museum, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-womens-monument">National Women's Monument</a>, the National Museum and the Oliewenhuis Art Museum, as well as Boyden Observatory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bloemfontein” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new state adopted a written constitution in April 1854 and kept <b>Bloemfontein</b>, founded as a British post in 1846, as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The republic kept the Union’s territory, its three capitals of Pretoria, Cape Town and <b>Bloemfontein</b>, and its rule over South West Africa, which the United Nations later declared illegal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union had three capitals: Pretoria for the government, Cape Town for parliament and <b>Bloemfontein</b> for the Appellate Division, a compromise South Africa still keeps.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>British forces took <b>Bloemfontein</b> in March 1900, and Britain proclaimed the annexation of the Orange Free State as the Orange River Colony in May 1900.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>A British Resident, Major Henry Warden, governed from <b>Bloemfontein</b> with little money and few troops.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-sovereignty">Orange River Sovereignty</a></li></ul></section>
